================================================================================
SCOPE — WHAT THIS LICENSE DOES AND DOES NOT COVER
================================================================================

This license covers the SOURCE CODE, documentation, evaluation harnesses and
gold-set annotations in this repository.

It does NOT cover, and cannot grant any rights to:

1. THIRD-PARTY ESG REPORTS under `backend/ESG_Reports/` (~84 MB of PDFs published
   by Shell, BP, Microsoft, Infosys and Tata Power). These remain the copyright
   of their respective publishers. They are present for local development only
   and are NOT redistributable under the terms above.

   >> Before publishing this repository or submitting it as a research artifact,
   >> replace these files with a fetch manifest (source URL + SHA-256 per report).
   >> That is both the licensing fix and better provenance practice, since a hash
   >> pins the exact document a result was computed from.

2. MODEL WEIGHTS pulled at runtime from HuggingFace (`bge-base-en-v1.5`,
   `distilbert-base-uncased-mnli`), which carry their own upstream licenses.

3. OUTPUT OF THIRD-PARTY APIs (NVIDIA NIM, Groq, OpenAI, Anthropic), which is
   governed by the terms of whichever provider generated it.

================================================================================
TWO THINGS TO CONFIRM BEFORE PUBLISHING
================================================================================

Added by the 2026-08-10 audit, which found no LICENSE file at all. Both of the
following are placeholders chosen so the repository is not left unlicensed —
neither is a decision this file can make for you.

  (a) COPYRIGHT HOLDER. "The ESGenuine authors" is deliberately generic. All 74
      commits are authored by `Ansh Bajpai <anshbajpai2005@gmail.com>`, but the
      repository is being operated by a different account, so the actual holder
      (individual? both authors? a university, if this is coursework or funded
      research?) could not be determined from the repository alone. Institutional
      affiliation often determines ownership — check before publishing.

  (b) LICENSE CHOICE. MIT is the common default for academic NLP artifacts and is
      accepted by every artifact-evaluation track. Apache-2.0 is the alternative
      worth considering: same permissions, plus an express patent grant and a
      patent-retaliation clause. If there is any prospect of commercialisation,
      or an institution with a technology-transfer office is involved, prefer
      Apache-2.0.
